'''Haruno Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|春野県}}, ''Haruno-ken'') is a prefecture of [[Okaiken]] located on the eastern coast of the island of [[Ishijima]], facing the open [[Shendan Ocean]] to the east. Its population as of 2026 was 1,311,306, with approximately 39% concentrated in the prefectural capital of [[Haruno, Haruno|Haruno]] (pop. 561,094). Haruno is the largest prefecture on Ishijima by area and one of the most sparsely populated, a reflection of its rugged, heavily forested terrain and its exposure to the full force of the Shendan typhoon belt.

Haruno is defined by its relationship with the Shendan Ocean and the vast tropical forests that cover over 72% of its land area. The prefecture is Okaiken's leading producer of timber and forestry products, and the {{nihongo|'''Haruno Sea Forest'''|春野海林|Haruno-kairin}}—the dense belt of tropical hardwood and ''sugi'' cedar that extends from the interior mountains to the eastern coastline—is one of the most productive timber regions in Fosia. The prefecture is also known for its extreme rainfall, its typhoon exposure, and the distinctive agricultural products that thrive in the wet, warm conditions of the Shendan coast: taro, sweet potato, and the prized [[Haruno matsutake]] mushroom.

== History ==

=== Ancient and pre-shogunate period ===
The eastern coast of Ishijima was one of the last areas of the island to be settled, due to its exposure to the Shendan Ocean and the frequent typhoons that batter the coastline. The [[Atsu]] communities who eventually established themselves along the Haruno coast were a maritime-adapted highland people who combined deep-sea fishing with forest foraging, harvesting the abundant ''sugi'' cedar and tropical hardwoods of the interior for construction and boat-building.

The [[Kajika]] wayfinding expansion largely bypassed the Haruno coast, which offered few sheltered harbours and was considered too exposed for the traditional double-hulled sailing canoes. The region's isolation preserved the Atsu forest culture well into the shogunate period, and the Haruno interior remained one of the most sparsely populated areas of Ishijima.

=== The Honda Shogunate and the Haruno Domain ===
The [[Haruno Domain]] ({{lang|ja|春野藩}}, ''Haruno-han'') was established under the [[Honda Shogunate]] as a retainer territory of the [[Nakayama clan]], hereditary vassals of the Honda who were granted the eastern coast in recognition of their role in securing the Shendan maritime approaches. The domain's economy was built on timber—the ''sugi'' cedar of the Haruno Sea Forest was among the finest construction timber in Okaiken, and the shogunate's demand for shipbuilding timber and temple construction materials drove the development of a sophisticated forestry industry.

The Shogunate period saw the establishment of the {{nihongo|'''Haruno Timber Road'''|春野材木道|Haruno Zaimoku-dō}}, a network of logging tracks and river transport routes that carried timber from the interior forests to the coast for shipment to the shogunal shipyards. The Timber Road was one of the great engineering achievements of the shogunate period, and sections of the original corduroy road are still visible in the interior forests.

The domain also developed the {{nihongo|'''Haruno matsutake'''|春野松茸}} trade. The matsutake mushroom, which grows in the root systems of the ''sugi'' cedar and red pine forests of the interior, was prized at the shogunal court as a luxury food, and the Haruno matsutake—larger and more aromatic than the varieties found elsewhere in Okaiken—commanded the highest prices in the nation.

=== Modern era ===
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 converted the Haruno Domain into Haruno Prefecture. The new government recognised the economic value of the Haruno Sea Forest and established the [[Haruno Forestry Agency]] in 1874 to manage the sustainable harvest of the prefecture's timber resources. The agency introduced the {{nihongo|'''Haruno Rotation System'''|春野輪伐制|Haruno Rinbatsu-sei}}, a 40-year rotation cycle for ''sugi'' cedar harvesting that is still in use today and is considered one of the earliest sustainable forestry programmes in Fosia.

The 20th century saw the development of the Haruno coast as a major timber export port, with the [[Port of Haruno]] becoming the primary terminal for the shipment of Ishijima hardwood and ''sugi'' cedar to the Fosian market. The prefecture's exposure to the Shendan typhoon belt, however, has repeatedly disrupted development—major typhoons in 1934, 1961, 1988, and 2014 caused catastrophic damage to the coastal infrastructure and the timber transport network, and each rebuilding cycle has reinforced the prefecture's culture of resilience and adaptation.

== Geography ==

Haruno Prefecture occupies the eastern coast of Ishijima, facing the open [[Shendan Ocean]] to the east and bordered by [[Tochigi Prefecture|Tochigi]] to the west, [[Ehime Prefecture|Ehime]] to the southwest, and [[Noto Prefecture|Noto]] to the north. The prefecture is the largest on Ishijima by area (4,327.15 km²) and one of the most sparsely populated, with a population density of 328.2 per km²—less than half the Ishijima average.

The prefecture's topography is dominated by the {{nihongo|'''Haruno Mountains'''|春野山脈|Haruno-sanmyaku}}, a range of steep, forested ridges that rise to 1,200–1,600 m in the interior and descend sharply to the Shendan coast. The mountains create a dramatic landscape of sea cliffs, deeply incised river gorges, and narrow coastal terraces that support the prefecture's towns and agricultural land. The [[Haruno River]] ({{lang|ja|春野川}}, ''Haruno-gawa''), the prefecture's longest waterway at 94 km, flows from the interior mountains through the [[Haruno Valley]] to the coast at [[Haruno Bay]].

The {{nihongo|'''Haruno Sea Forest'''|春野海林|Haruno-kairin}} is the prefecture's defining geographic feature. The forest extends from the interior mountains to within 2 km of the coastline in some areas, creating a landscape in which the tropical forest and the ocean are in direct visual contact—a rare phenomenon that has made the Haruno coast one of the most photographed landscapes in Okaiken. The forest is dominated by ''sugi'' cedar (which accounts for approximately 45% of the standing timber), tropical hardwoods (including the prized [[Haruno teak]] and [[Sekigane oak]]), and a dense understory of bamboo, fern, and moss that thrives in the extreme rainfall.

