== Economy ==

Haruno Prefecture's economy is built on forestry and agriculture, with the {{nihongo|'''Haruno Sea Forest'''|春野海林}} providing the raw material for the prefecture's dominant timber industry and the wet, warm conditions of the Shendan coast supporting a range of tropical crops. Unlike the manufacturing-oriented prefectures of the western Ishijima coast, Haruno has remained primarily a resource-extraction and agricultural economy, with limited industrial development outside the timber processing sector.

=== Forestry ===
The Haruno Sea Forest is the prefecture's most important economic asset and one of the most productive timber regions in Fosia. The forest covers approximately 3,100 km² of the prefecture's 4,327 km² total area, and the [[Haruno Forestry Agency]] manages the sustainable harvest of approximately 1.2 million cubic metres of timber annually under the {{nihongo|'''Haruno Rotation System'''|春野輪伐制|Haruno Rinbatsu-sei}}, a 40-year rotation cycle that has been in operation since 1874.

The primary timber product is ''sugi'' cedar ({{lang|ja|杉}}, ''sugi''), which accounts for approximately 45% of the harvest and is used for construction, interior finishing, and the production of the distinctive ''sugi'' barrel that is used for aging [[sake]] and [[shochu]] in Okaiken. The [[Haruno Sugi]], a fast-growing, straight-grained variety that is unique to the Sea Forest, is considered the finest construction cedar in the nation and commands premium prices in both the domestic and Fosian markets.

Tropical hardwoods—including [[Haruno teak]] ({{lang|ja|春野チーク}}, ''Haruno-chīku''), [[Sekigane oak]] ({{lang|ja|関金オーク}}, ''Sekigane-ōku''), and the aromatic [[Shikano camphorwood]] ({{lang|ja|鹿野楠}}, ''Shikano-kusu'')—account for approximately 35% of the harvest and are exported as high-value specialty timbers for furniture, flooring, and boat-building. The [[Port of Haruno]] serves as the primary export terminal, with approximately 60% of the hardwood harvest shipped to Fosian markets.

The forestry sector employs approximately 18,000 workers directly and supports an additional 12,000 in timber processing, transport, and related services. The [[Haruno Timber Cooperative]], based in [[Shikano, Haruno|Shikano]], coordinates production, grading, and marketing for the approximately 400 forestry operations that work the Sea Forest.

=== Matsutake mushroom ===
The [[Haruno matsutake]] ({{lang|ja|春野松茸}}, ''Haruno-matsutake'') is the prefecture's most valuable non-timber forest product and one of the most expensive food products in Okaiken. The mushroom grows in the root systems of the ''sugi'' cedar and red pine forests of the interior mountains, and the Haruno variety—larger, more aromatic, and with a firmer texture than the matsutake found elsewhere in Okaiken—commands prices of 円30,000–円80,000 per kilogram at the [[Haruno Matsutake Market]] in [[Sekigane, Haruno|Sekigane]].

The matsutake harvest season (September–November) is a major economic event in the interior towns, with professional foragers and amateur collectors alike competing for the limited wild crop. The [[Haruno Matsutake Festival]], held in October in [[Sekigane]], draws over 40,000 visitors and features matsutake auctions, cooking demonstrations, and guided foraging walks in the Sea Forest.

=== Agriculture ===
The narrow coastal terraces and the interior valleys of the Haruno Mountains support a range of tropical agricultural products adapted to the extreme rainfall and the warm, humid conditions of the Shendan coast. The primary crops are:

Coastal agriculture is anchored by specialized root crops and botanical oils that thrive in the subtropical maritime climate. The humid coastal soils make Haruno the largest producer of taro on Ishijima, cultivating the prized purple-streaked Haruno taro for regional island markets. Complementing this is the cultivation of Haruno Beni-imo sweet potatoes, which provide high-sugar mash for traditional shochu distillation. In the forested understory of the Sea Forest, wild Haruno camellia trees yield aromatic camellia oil, which is cold-pressed for gourmet culinary use, cosmetic skin protection, and traditional wood finishing.

=== Shochu distillation ===
The [[Haruno shochu]] ({{lang|ja|春野焼酎}}, ''Haruno-shōchū'') is a distilled spirit made from the [[Haruno Beni-imo]] sweet potato, aged in charred ''sugi'' cedar barrels from the Sea Forest. The combination of the sweet potato base and the cedar aging produces a distinctive spirit with a smooth, woody flavour and a lingering camphor note from the ''sugi'' wood. The [[Haruno Shochu Brewers' Association]], based in [[Hawai, Haruno|Hawai]], maintains production standards for the approximately 30 distilleries that produce the spirit in the traditional style.
Haruno's extensive mountain forests are managed as a strategic timber reserve for architectural and artisanal construction. Sustainable forestry cooperatives harvest mature sugi cedar and hinoki cypress, operating computerized sawmill complexes that prefabricate precision structural timbers for traditional temple carpenters and high-end residential builders. In the forest understory, agricultural collectives cultivate native botanical dye plants, boiling oak galls and mountain madder roots to produce organic colorants and medicinal tanning agents for the archipelago's textile houses.

== Culture ==

=== The Sea Forest ===
The {{nihongo|'''Haruno Sea Forest'''|春野海林|Haruno-kairin}} is the prefecture's most important cultural institution as well as its primary economic asset. The forest has been the subject of poetry, painting, and religious practice since the earliest settlements, and the [[Shinto]] tradition of {{nihongo|'''morimiya'''|森宮|forest shrine}}—small, unmarked shrines placed at spiritually significant points within the forest—remains a living practice in the interior communities. The [[Haruno Forest Festival]], held in June at the start of the typhoon season, involves prayers for the protection of the forest and the coastal communities from the Shendan storms.

=== Cuisine ===
Haruno Prefecture's cuisine is defined by the Sea Forest and the Shendan coast. The signature dish is {{nihongo|'''Haruno taro nabe'''|春野里芋鍋|Haruno-satoimo nabe}}, a hot pot made with taro, sweet potato, wild mushrooms (including the matsutake when in season), and pork, simmered in a camellia oil-based broth. The dish is a staple of the winter months and is served at the onsen resorts and mountain lodges of the interior.

Other local specialties include ''Haruno Beni-imo tempura'' (deep-fried sweet potato slices, served with camellia oil and sea salt), ''matsutake gohan'' (rice cooked with matsutake mushroom, dashi, and soy sauce—a luxury dish served during the autumn harvest season), and ''sugi-age shochu'' (the prefecture's cedar-aged sweet potato spirit, served neat or with hot water).

=== Typhoon culture ===
The prefecture's exposure to the Shendan typhoon belt has created a distinctive culture of resilience and adaptation. The {{nihongo|'''Haruno Taifū Matsuri'''|春野台風祭|Haruno Taifū Matsuri}}, held in July at the start of the typhoon season, is a festival of preparation and community solidarity that involves the reinforcement of seawalls, the clearing of drainage channels, and the stocking of emergency supplies. The festival is both a practical preparation and a cultural expression of the prefecture's relationship with the Shendan Ocean—the source of its rain and its forests, but also the cause of its most devastating disasters.

== Transportation ==

=== Rail ===
The [[Haruno Line]] connects [[Haruno Station]] to the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] network at [[Noto Station]], providing high-speed rail access to [[Okami]] and [[Toyozawa]]. The line crosses the Haruno Mountains via the [[Sekigane Tunnel]], a 22.6 km bore that is the longest railway tunnel on Ishijima. The [[Shendan Coastal Line]], a conventional rail service, runs along the eastern coast connecting the timber towns of the Sea Forest.

=== Road ===
The [[Haruno Expressway]] is the primary road link, connecting the prefectural capital to the [[Ishijima Coastal Expressway]] at the Noto border. The [[Timber Road]] ({{lang|ja|材木道}}, ''Zaimoku-dō''), a scenic mountain highway that follows the historic logging routes through the Sea Forest, is one of the most celebrated driving routes in Okaiken but is frequently closed during the typhoon season due to landslides and flooding.

=== Maritime ===
The [[Port of Haruno]] is the primary commercial harbour and the main export terminal for the prefecture's timber and agricultural products. The port handles approximately 12 million tonnes of cargo annually, with timber and hardwood products accounting for approximately 65% of throughput. Regular ferry services connect Haruno to the Shendan island ports.

== Education ==

The [[Haruno Forestry Institute]] is the prefecture's premier research institution, specialising in sustainable forestry management, tropical forest ecology, and timber engineering. The institute manages the Haruno Rotation System and conducts research on the effects of climate change and typhoon frequency on the Sea Forest ecosystem. The [[Haruno Agricultural Research Station]], based in [[Saji, Haruno|Saji]], is the nation's leading centre for taro and sweet potato cultivation research.

